prompt.text:
Implement a new, self-contained TypeScript module exporting exactly this API:

```typescript
export type Setting = null | boolean | number | string | readonly Setting[] | Settings;
export interface Settings { readonly [key: string]: Setting | undefined }
export function mergeSettings(layers: readonly Settings[]): Settings;
```

`mergeSettings` must return a new settings tree. Process layers from first to last, so a later property overrides an earlier property. When a later value is a settings object, recursively combine it with the earlier value if that value is also a settings object; otherwise combine it with an empty object. Arrays are atomic replacement values rather than index-merged values. `undefined` is an explicit value and must overwrite an earlier property.

Only own enumerable string properties participate. Empty input returns an empty object. Every object and array reachable from the result must be newly allocated, including values appearing only in one layer, so neither later input mutation nor result mutation can affect the other. Repeated references in the input need not retain shared identity in the output. Inputs must never be mutated.

At runtime, valid values are `null`, booleans, strings, finite numbers, arrays of valid values, and plain objects whose prototype is either `Object.prototype` or `null`; plain-object properties may additionally be `undefined`. Cycles, non-finite numbers, array elements equal to `undefined`, class instances, functions, symbols, and bigints are malformed and must cause a `TypeError`. Shared acyclic references are valid. Property names such as `__proto__` must be handled as ordinary own properties without changing an output object's prototype. Return one complete source file suitable for strict CommonJS compilation targeting ES2022.

